# Error Handling

## RequestError — backend

Throw `RequestError(message, code)` from REST actions. `furystack/rest-action-use-request-error` lint rule forbids plain `throw new Error(...)` in actions.

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 400 | Validation / bad request |
| 401 | Authentication required |
| 403 | Forbidden (authorized but not permitted) |
| 404 | Not found |
| 409 | Conflict (resource already exists) |
| 500 | Server error |

```typescript
export const CreateUserAction: RequestAction<CreateUserEndpoint> = async ({ injector, getBody }) => {
  const logger = getLogger(injector).withScope('CreateUserAction')
  const body = await getBody()

  try {
    const existing = await store.find({ filter: { email: { $eq: body.email } } })
    if (existing.count > 0) throw new RequestError('User with this email already exists', 409)

    const user = await store.add(body)
    return JsonResult(user)
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof RequestError) throw error
    await logger.error({ message: 'Failed to create user', data: { error } })
    throw new RequestError('Failed to create user', 500)
  }
}
```

Always log with context but return user-friendly messages — never leak stack traces or internal details to clients.

## Result types over throwing for expected failures

For batch / loop operations where multiple expected outcomes exist, return a discriminated union instead of throwing. Reserve `throw RequestError(...)` for REST validation, programming bugs, and data integrity issues. See `BACKEND_PATTERNS.mdc` for the full pattern.

## Observable error states — frontend services

Expose `error` and `isLoading` as observables on services. Components subscribe and render accordingly. The `Impl` class + `defineService` token wrapper is the canonical pi-rat shape — see `CODE_STYLE.mdc`.

```typescript
class UserServiceImpl implements Disposable {
  public error = new ObservableValue<string>('')
  public isLoading = new ObservableValue(false)
  public users = new ObservableValue<User[]>([])

  constructor(private readonly apiClient: IdentityApiClient) {}

  public async loadUsers() {
    this.isLoading.setValue(true)
    this.error.setValue('')
    try {
      const users = await this.apiClient.getUsers()
      this.users.setValue(users)
    } catch (err) {
      this.error.setValue(err instanceof Error ? err.message : 'Failed to load users')
    } finally {
      this.isLoading.setValue(false)
    }
  }

  public [Symbol.dispose](): void {
    this.error[Symbol.dispose]()
    this.isLoading[Symbol.dispose]()
    this.users[Symbol.dispose]()
  }
}
```

In Cache load functions, errors are translated to a `failed` cache state automatically — see `CACHE_HANDLING.mdc`.

## User-friendly error messages

Translate raw errors before showing them. Reusable utility:

```typescript
export const getUserFriendlyErrorMessage = (error: unknown): string => {
  if (error instanceof RequestError) return error.message

  if (error instanceof Error) {
    if (/Network Error|fetch/.test(error.message)) return 'Unable to connect to the server. Please check your internet connection.'
    if (/401|Unauthorized/.test(error.message)) return 'Your session has expired. Please log in again.'
    if (/403|Forbidden/.test(error.message)) return 'You do not have permission to perform this action.'
    if (/404|Not Found/.test(error.message)) return 'The requested resource was not found.'
    if (/500|Internal Server Error/.test(error.message)) return 'Server error. Please try again later.'
    return error.message
  }
  return 'Something went wrong. Please try again.'
}
```

## Retry with exponential backoff

For transient failures (network blips, 5xx). Cap the delay so no single retry stalls forever.

```typescript
export async function retryWithBackoff<T>(
  fn: () => Promise<T>,
  maxRetries = 3,
  initialDelay = 1000,
): Promise<T> {
  let lastError: unknown
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {
    try { return await fn() }
    catch (error) {
      lastError = error
      if (attempt < maxRetries) {
        const delay = Math.min(initialDelay * 2 ** attempt, 30000)
        await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, delay))
      }
    }
  }
  throw lastError
}
```

## Graceful degradation

Provide fallback content when data fails to load — don't show a broken UI.

```typescript
const UserAvatar = Shade<{ userId: string }>({
  customElementName: 'user-avatar',
  render: ({ props, injector, useObservable }) => {
    const userService = injector.get(UsersService)
    const [state] = useObservable('user', userService.userCache.getObservable(props.userId))

    const isMissing = !state || state.status === 'failed' || state.status === 'loading'
    if (isMissing) return <div class="avatar avatar-fallback">?</div>
    return <div class="avatar">{state.value.name.charAt(0).toUpperCase()}</div>
  },
})
```

## Form validation

Client-side: catch obvious problems early to give immediate feedback. Server-side: still authoritative — `Validate(...)` schemas + explicit checks (see `REST_ACTION_VALIDATION.mdc`).

```typescript
const handleSubmit = async (data: FormPayload) => {
  if (data.password !== data.confirmPassword) {
    error.setValue('Passwords do not match')
    return
  }
  // ...
}
```
